## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-world integration of dynamic forms, streaming data, and memoization under per-project scope.

## Key Concepts
- **useSyncExternalStore** for streaming form data
- **Memoized selectors** with composite keys (projectId, formId, step)
- **Dynamic forms** with field-level memoization
- **react-window** virtualization for long field lists
- **Optimistic updates** with per-field rollback on server rejection
- Proper **cleanup** on project switch and stream unsubscription

## Code Example
```typescript
function useFormStream(projectId: string, formId: string, step: number) {
  const store = useRef(getPerProjectStore(projectId));
  const selector = useMemo(
    () => createVisibleFieldsSelector(projectId, formId, step),
    [projectId, formId, step]
  );

  return useSyncExternalStore(
    store.current.subscribe,
    () => selector(store.current.getState()),
    () => selector(store.current.getServerState())
  );
}

const FieldRow = React.memo(({ field, value, onChange }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <FieldInput field={field} value={value} onChange={onChange} />
    </div>
  );
});

function FormStep({ projectId, formId, step }) {
  const fields = useFormStream(projectId, formId, step);

  return (
    <FixedSizeList height={600} itemData={fields}>
      {({ index, style }) => (
        <FieldRow 
          field={fields[index]} 
          style={style}
        />
      )}
    </FixedSizeList>
  );
}
```

## Implementation Notes
1. The selector recomputes only when (projectId, formId, step) changes
2. FieldRow memoization prevents re-renders when unrelated fields update
3. react-window renders only visible rows, reducing DOM nodes
4. On server rejection, restore the previous field value from a snapshot taken before the optimistic update
5. Unsubscribe from the stream and clear selectors in a cleanup effect when projectId changes

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates advanced JavaScript patterns including per-key caching, Promise deduplication, TTL-based eviction, and reactive update patterns commonly needed in frontend applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Closures and per-key maps**: Isolated state management for each cached key
- **Promise deduplication**: Avoiding redundant network requests for concurrent loads of the same key
- **TTL-based eviction**: Time-to-live expiration for cache entries
- **Change notifications**: Listener pattern for broadcasting data updates
- **Error handling**: Graceful handling of fetch failures and cache misses

## Code Example
```javascript
function createDataLoader(fetchFn, ttlMs) {
  const cache = new Map();

  function getEntry(key) {
    if (!cache.has(key)) {
      cache.set(key, { promise: null, data: null, expiry: 0, listeners: [] });
    }
    return cache.get(key);
  }

  const notify = (key, data, error) => {
    const entry = cache.get(key);
    if (entry?.listeners.length) {
      entry.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(data, error));
    }
  };

  return {
    load(key) {
      const entry = getEntry(key);
      const now = Date.now();

      if (entry.data && entry.expiry > now) {
        return Promise.resolve(entry.data);
      }

      if (entry.promise) {
        return entry.promise;
      }

      entry.promise = fetchFn(key)
        .then(data => {
          entry.data = data;
          entry.expiry = now + ttlMs;
          entry.promise = null;
          notify(key, data, null);
          return data;
        })
        .catch(err => {
          entry.promise = null;
          notify(key, null, err);
          throw err;
        });

      return entry.promise;
    },

    invalidate(key) {
      const entry = cache.get(key);
      if (entry) {
        entry.data = null;
        entry.expiry = 0;
        entry.promise = null;
      }
    },

    onChange(key, cb) {
      const entry = getEntry(key);
      entry.listeners.push(cb);
      return () => {
        entry.listeners = entry.listeners.filter(l => l !== cb);
      };
    }
  };
}
```

## Usage Example
```javascript
const loader = createDataLoader(fetchUser, 5000);

// Two different keys
loader.onChange('user:1', (data) => console.log('User 1 updated:', data));
loader.onChange('user:2', (data) => console.log('User 2 updated:', data));

// Concurrent loads for same key are deduplicated
loader.load('user:1').then(console.log);
loader.load('user:1').then(console.log); // Reuses in-flight request

// Invalidate and reload
loader.invalidate('user:1');
loader.load('user:1');
```

## Eviction Strategies
- **TTL-based**: Automatic expiration after ttlMs (implemented above)
- **LRU**: Evict least recently used entries when cache size exceeds limit
- **Manual**: Explicit invalidate() calls

## Error Handling
- Failed fetches propagate errors to all waiters via rejected Promise
- Listeners receive error state for reactive UI updates
- In-flight promises are cleared on error to allow retries

